WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.120
- My objective is to make
it financially unsustainable

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.624
for them to continue to
kill black people unjustly.

00:00:09.624 --> 00:00:12.207
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.700
The film, "Civil" deals
with many meanings.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.560
Obviously, I'm a civil trial
lawyer, a civil rights lawyer,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.830
not a criminal justice lawyer.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.490
I don't have any power to arrest a person

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.680
and to put them in jail

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.380
and hold them criminally accountable,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.872
and I think Nadia Hallgren
did a very important job

00:00:41.872 --> 00:00:43.590
showing the distinction.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.860
- I was home in May in lockdown

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.770
just like the rest of
America and, you know,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.860
my last film "Becoming"
came out early in May

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.290
and three weeks later,
George Floyd was murdered.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.150
You know, as a black filmmaker

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.200
I'm just sitting home thinking
I have to be doing something.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.220
I have to be out there.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:03.390
I have to be telling a story.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.010
This is what I do.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.110
And I was really scratching my head

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.360
just trying to figure
out where do I get in?

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.440
Where's my jump off?

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.870
How do I begin to tell the story

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.000
of this historical
moment that's happening?

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.913
Shortly after that, the phone
rings and it's Kenya Barris.

00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:21.540
And he tells me, "I'm
working on this project

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.813
with Ben Crump, and we're looking

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:25.800
for a documentary filmmaker

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.180
to basically jump in with him right now.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.840
It's all going down and
we wanna make this film."

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.190
And he's like, "Are you interested?"

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.610
And it was just like everything
that I had put out there

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.130
and had hoped for had just came to me.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.424
I met Ben very quickly on a Zoom,

00:01:43.424 --> 00:01:47.280
and I knew that that was the
next thing that I had to do.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.490
- I do want to pay the utmost respect

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.480
to Kenya Barris and Roger Ross Williams.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.340
Incredible producers.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.070
Roger has such gravitas

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.280
when it comes to the
documentary film space.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.700
And then to Kenya Barris,
you know, this young man

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.330
who's trying to project
the black experience

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.703
of America on to the screen.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.790
- All I did was pack my camera
bag and get on the road.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.040
I had no idea what I
was getting myself into

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.110
when I joined Attorney Crump.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.280
What I immediately saw
was just this person

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.050
who had an incredible warmth
to him, sense of humor,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.710
sort of this personality that
I think was really unexpected

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:35.850
that we often don't think of

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.830
when we think of civil rights lawyers.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.590
So as a filmmaker that really
enjoys character study,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:45.090
I knew that that would really
draw an audience into him,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.980
and his story, and his fight.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.290
And so that was a really
important part to me

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.480
to make sure that that really shined

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.370
amongst all the other
things that he was doing,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.020
but to stay connected to him

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.480
as a very, very unique individual.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.564
- And watching this documentary,

00:02:59.564 --> 00:03:04.564
it is a salute to the
genius of Nadia Hallgren.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.470
I had things revealed to me
that I was not even aware of.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:14.100
I tried to compartmentalize all of this,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.460
and not give way to emotions.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.870
But what she kept a lot of was

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.870
after I would talk to family
members and different people,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.370
I would find myself rubbing my head,

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.220
rubbing my temples, and so forth,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.220
and I never was aware
that I did that so much.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.690
It was almost like a
physical manifestation

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.800
of the mental anguish I was dealing with,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.040
and dealing with this tragic loss of life.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.482
- You know, we have a scene in the film

00:03:48.482 --> 00:03:50.700
we call banking while black,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.490
and there's this young mother Shera Brown

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:57.720
who goes to cash a $90
check and gets accused

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.810
of committing fraud, and the
police get called on her.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.530
And it's so sad, and that experience

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.050
lefts her unable to buy
milk for her babies.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.600
When I was filming a scene like that,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.791
tears were just falling
into my View Finder.

00:04:11.791 --> 00:04:14.850
You know, we have stories
of people being murdered

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.733
and things like that in the
film that are also deeply sad,

00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:20.460
but that story reminded
me of my own mother

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.460
and experiences that I've had in my life.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.400
I just think that having
that very personal connection

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.950
to the people in the
film and the experiences

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.930
allowed me to tell the story in a way

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.480
that I hope is very nuanced

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.940
and deeply resonates with our audiences

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.860
because we know that that's something

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.450
that not everyone will experience,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.780
but everyone needs to know
is something that happens.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.720
- I'm often asked, being Crumb,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.375
how do you deal with the mental
strain, the mental anguish

00:04:50.375 --> 00:04:54.750
of dealing with such
heartache, such tragedy,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.320
such death on a constant basis?

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.610
And I'm a student of
history so I look back at

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.160
what my personal hero, Thurgood Marshall,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.460
said about dealing with
constant injustices.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.720
He said, "You have to stay
focused on the mission

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.780
because if you are not
focused on the mission,

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.400
if you become emotional

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.370
and you can't be strong to stay focused,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.870
then you can't prevent future
injustices from happening.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.755
And so when these families
who have experienced

00:05:27.755 --> 00:05:32.280
the worst loss imaginable,
what you are trying to do

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.490
is be strong for them too in that moment

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.466
when they need somebody to
try to help them make sense

00:05:39.466 --> 00:05:43.710
of something that makes
no sense whatsoever."

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.383
And so I just try to focus on the mission.

00:05:47.439 --> 00:05:50.220
(upbeat music)

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.454
- I was very fortunate to have

00:05:52.454 --> 00:05:55.290
one of the most talented edit teams

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.330
I think working in documentary filmmaking.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:01.020
We had Lindsay Utz, Enat
Sidi, and Nathan Punwar.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.820
I had some deep conversations with them

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.986
about the experience that I had with Ben,

00:06:04.986 --> 00:06:07.200
and what was important to me to highlight

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.040
in terms of his personality
and really who he was.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.790
So when we went in to craft our story,

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.190
some of the first things we
did was pull those moments.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.580
Knowing that this is a very
heavy film, it's very emotional.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.650
And what would help bring levity

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:26.820
to some of these deeply
tragic stories was Ben.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.110
We tried a lot of things, you know,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.820
it's all like one big experiment

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.440
that one day just works, you know?

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.040
And you just hope it works
within your edit schedule.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.140
- I'm in the public's eye so much,

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.080
but that's in my professional life

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.090
on a very specific mission.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.700
It's another thing entirely for me

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.510
to be the subject of the camera lens.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:53.510
Not just my work, but my
personal life, my family life,

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:57.840
and so it was a big decision,

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.460
but in talking with Kenya
Barris, our executive producer,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.220
and then getting to meet Nadia Hallgren.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:10.220
This just young genius behind the camera.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.944
They made me feel a little more trusting

00:07:13.944 --> 00:07:18.180
to put my life on center stage.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:23.010
With all of that said,
it still was concerning

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:27.000
because a lot of what
I do is a safety issue,

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.790
and I'm okay with it being me.

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.150
But to have my family and
the people you love most

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.870
now become known and then possibly have

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:41.870
a lot of the insults or anything
was very concerning to me.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:46.590
But Nadia promised me that
she would be very careful.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:50.070
So I did it because I
understood the mission,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:54.030
and the mission is we have
to speak truth to power

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:55.830
not only in the court of law,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.035
but more importantly in the
court of public opinion.

00:07:59.035 --> 00:08:02.310
(upbeat music)

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.980
- Ben always is talking about
speaking truth to power,

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.790
and I think for me as a
documentary filmmaker,

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.844
you know, I'm an observational
documentary filmmaker

00:08:10.844 --> 00:08:14.420
and I think that what I hope
we're able to do with this film

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.850
is without having to add anything extra

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:19.350
to just what was happening

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.230
that people can really see these stories,

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:25.824
and the powers that be
are able to understand

00:08:25.824 --> 00:08:27.270
more of what's happening.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:30.840
I mean, what we try to
do is take these moments

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.910
that Ben has especially with families

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:34.449
that he's interacting with,

00:08:34.449 --> 00:08:36.540
and show what we don't see on the news

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.230
and show the ripple
effects of these stories

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.390
that just feel like they
cycle in so quickly,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.760
we almost aren't even
able to keep up with them.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.700
I know that we have this
incredible global audience

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.700
on Netflix to share this film with,

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.030
and that's something that
I'm really grateful for.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:57.220
The movement that
happened in 2020 and 2021

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:58.920
was a global movement.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.320
We show in the film that there were people

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.470
that took to the streets
in South Korea, in Austria,

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.740
and places that you may not even expect,

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.050
but it was that global mobilization

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:13.470
that really put the pressure
on in America to say

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.470
you need to look at this, you
need to see what's happening.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:20.850
And so we pay an homage to
that global group of people

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.860
who like stood up for us, you know,

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:25.080
and like connected with our experiences.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.720
I'm excited for people to
see that and see themselves

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.870
in this film globally, and just to have

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.510
a deeper understanding
of black life in America,

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:38.430
and how deeply woven racism is
in every corner of our lives.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.470
Looking at what Attorney Ben Crump does,

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.127
and like he says in the film,

00:09:42.127 --> 00:09:43.950
"Get into the arena and do something.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.510
We can all do something."

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.010
You know, I really encourage people

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.890
to take that as a call
to action and be like,

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:51.510
cool, I could do something too.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:55.620
- It is my hope that people think about

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.050
the Netflix documentary, "Civil,"

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:02.460
for exactly what the word denotes.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.080
Now more than ever is that
we champion tolerance,

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:12.080
that we champion humanity,
that we champion civility,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:19.590
and being civil versus
resulting to violence.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:22.920
We have to show our children

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.920
that there is humanity in all of us,

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:32.104
and we should show
respect to that humanity.

00:10:32.104 --> 00:10:34.687
(upbeat music)

